From: chasey-dev <ellenyoung0912@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] mtk_hwifi: INIT_LIST_HEAD the dummy netdev's napi_list

The first netif_napi_add() on trans->napi_dev panics on 6.18:

  Unable to handle kernel paging request at virtual address ffffffffffffffffc
  pc : netif_napi_add_weight_locked+0xe4/0x2f4
  Call trace:
   netif_napi_add_weight_locked
   pci_dma_init_device+0xcc/0x530 [mtk_pci]
   mtk_bus_init_device [mtk_hwifi]
   connac_if_start [connac_if]
   hwifi_start [mt_wifi]
   mt_wifi_open
   ...

netif_napi_add_weight_locked() calls netif_napi_dev_list_add(), which
walks dev->napi_list with list_for_each_entry() before inserting the new
napi.  dev->napi_list is only INIT_LIST_HEAD()ed by alloc_netdev_mqs();
init_dummy_netdev() sets reg_state and the link state bits and nothing
else.  Because napi_dev is an embedded struct net_device inside the
kzalloc()ed pci_trans, the list head stays all-zero, so
list_for_each_entry() computes

  container_of(NULL, struct napi_struct, dev_list)

i.e. -offsetof(struct napi_struct, dev_list).  In 6.18 the layout is

  u32              napi_id;
  struct list_head dev_list;

so the bogus node sits at -0x180 and reading pos->napi_id faults at
0xfffffffc -- exactly the address in the oops.  netif_napi_del() would
walk the same broken list on the teardown path.

Older kernels never touched dev->napi_list in netif_napi_add(), which is
why this only shows up now.  Initialising the list head is version
independent and harmless on the old ones.

Signed-off-by: chasey-dev <ellenyoung0912@gmail.com>
---
--- a/wlan_hwifi/bus/mtk_pci_dma.c
+++ b/wlan_hwifi/bus/mtk_pci_dma.c
@@ -2197,6 +2197,26 @@
 
 	mt_init_dummy_netdev(&trans->napi_dev);
 
+	/*
+	 * v6.12+ keeps a per-net_device NAPI list: netif_napi_add() walks it
+	 * with list_for_each_entry() before inserting, netif_napi_del() unlinks
+	 * from it.  init_dummy_netdev() only sets reg_state/state and never
+	 * INIT_LIST_HEAD()s dev->napi_list -- the kernel expects the device to
+	 * have come through alloc_netdev().  Ours is an embedded
+	 * struct net_device in a kzalloc()ed pci_trans, so napi_list.next stays
+	 * NULL and the first netif_napi_add() does
+	 *
+	 *   container_of(NULL, struct napi_struct, dev_list)
+	 *
+	 * which lands at -offsetof(dev_list) and reads napi_id (the u32 right
+	 * before dev_list) at 0xfffffffc:
+	 *
+	 *   Unable to handle kernel paging request at ffffffffffffffffc
+	 *   pc : netif_napi_add_weight_locked+0xe4/0x2f4
+	 *   pci_dma_init_device+0xcc/0x530 [mtk_pci]
+	 */
+	INIT_LIST_HEAD(&trans->napi_dev.napi_list);
+
 	ret = pci_dma_sw_init_tx_queue(trans);
 	if (ret) {
 		dev_err(to_device(trans), "%s(): TXQ init failed, ret = %d\n",
